rimary value is to identify individuals  who are a threat to our interests either  through terrorism or espionage or  reasons of that kind there's a wider  role for the intelligence agencies which  is to collect information which might  indicate what heads of governments that  are unfriendly may be up to maybe  planning that's much more difficult  that's much more difficult sometimes our  agencies do get it wrong for example on  on the falklands that was a huge uh  error a huge area of incompetence the  bletchley park however is the world when  britain broke the german naval code that  is the world's best  intelligence achievement of all time and  where do you think it went wrong  with russia you were of course in the  government for the  early part of that  where do we take the wrong turning in  being true complacent or too trusting no  i think what people overlook is that the  what happened at the end of the cold war  wasn't one event  it was several events it was the end of  the cold war it was the end of communism  versus capitalism but most important in  the current context it was the end of  europe's last empire the russian empire  and what putin has never  acknowledged or be prepared to accept  because russian leaders believe that to  protect russia they have to control what  they call the near abroad the countries  around them and that's not acceptable in  the modern europe and this is the  dilemma we face in europe putin with a  lot of support in russia believes that  they have a right to interfere with the  independence of ukraine you say that now  but i mean i remember  going on visits to russia with british  prime ministers who saw him as a  continuation after yeltsin  gorbachev of leaders who wanted to be  friendly with that that's what clearly  doesn't well and we made a big mistake  in the first period of putin's rule  he brought back stability yeltsin's rule  had been rather chaotic rather anarchic  putin represented stability he was also  fortunate 